      desc = Mycena haematopus (bleeding fairy helmet, burgundydrop bonnet, bleeding Mycena). A red-brown mushroom with a lighter stem and white gills. Found in North America and Europe, though also reported in Japan and Venezuela.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Lilac" || Base == "LilacTort" || Base == "Fawn" || Base == "FawnTort") && (White == "NoWhite" || White == "LowWhite") && Albino != "Point") && (NorthAm || MidAm || SouthAm || NorthAf || SouthAf)]
      desc = Mycena holoporphyra. A warm grey-brown mushroom with a thin stem. First described from Cuba, it is also found in Trinidad, Africa, Mexico, and Central America and South America.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(Base == "White" || (Base == "Cream" && (White == "HighWhite" || Albino != "Normal"))) && (SEastAs)]
      desc = Mycena illuminans. White mushrooms with a pale cream top. Found on Jawa, Indonesia.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena|Bonnetcap}^[((Base == "Fawn" || Base == "Chocolate" || Base == "FawnTort" || Base == "ChocolateTort") && (Albino != "Normal")) && (NorthAm || Europe || NorthAf || WestAs || EastAs || SEastAs || Japan || NewZeal || Islands)]
      desc = Mycena inclinata (clustered bonnet, oak-stump bonnet cap). A pale mushroom with a brown stem and brown-tipped cap. In eastern North America, it is abundant in the area bounded by Nova Scotia, Ontario, Manitoba, Missouri, North Carolina, and New York. It has been found in Oregon, but the species appears to be generally rare along the Pacific Coast. The range of the fungus also includes Europe, the Canary Islands, North Africa, East Siberia, Japan, Malesia, Turkey, and New Zealand.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(Base == "Black" && Agouti == "Solid" && Albino == "Normal") && (Islands)]
      desc = Mycena indigotica. A black and white mushroom found in Taiwan.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena|Parasol}^[(Base == "Blue" && (White == "HighWhite" || Albino == "Point")) && (SouthAm || Australia || NewZeal || Islands)]
      desc = Mycena interrupta (pixie's parasol). A pale mushroom with a blue cap. Found in Australia, New Zealand, New Caledonia and Chile. In Australia, it is found in Victoria, Tasmania, New South Wales, South Australia, and Queensland, where its distribution is limited to Lamington National Park.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(((Base == "Chocolate" && Agouti != "Solid") || Base == "ChocolateTort") && (White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal" || Albino == "Sepia") && (!Silver || Agouti == "Solid")) && (Australia)]
      desc = Mycena kuurkacea. An orange-brown mushroom with a dark brown stem. Found in New South Wales, Australia.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(Base == "White") && (Japan)]
      desc = Mycena lazulina. A tiny mostly white fungus from southwestern Japan.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(Base == "Red" && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Mycena leaiana (orange mycena, Lea's mycena). An orange fungus growing on trees throughout the eastern and central United States and Canada.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(((Agouti == "Tabby" && !Silver && Gold != "None" && Base != "Red" && Base != "Cream") || (Base == "BlueTort" || Base == "LilacTort")) && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (Australia || NewZeal)]
      desc = Mycena leaiana var. australis (orange mycena, Lea's mycena). A yellow fungus with a dark grey cap found in Australia and New Zealand.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Lilac" || Base == "LilacTort" || Base == "Fawn" || Base == "FawnTort") && (White == "MidWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) && (NorthAm || SouthAm || Europe || British || EastAs || Islands)]
      desc = Mycena leptocephala (nitrous bonnet). A warm grey-brown mushroom with a white stem. In North America, the fungus is found in Canada (British Columbia, Manitoba, Nova Scotia), to Washington and south to California and North Carolina. In South America, the mushroom has been collected in Venezuela. It also grows in the Archipelago of the Recherche, off the southern coast of Western Australia. In Europe, it is known from Britain, Finland, The Netherlands, Norway, and Spain. It has also been found in various locations in Asia: the Vindhya Range of India; the Gwangneung Forest Museum in the Korea National Arboretum; and the alpine zone of Changbai Mountain Nature Reserve, Jilin Province, China. The fungus is also known from Arctic and Alpine regions such as Iceland, Greenland, and the Murmansk region.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(((Base == "Red" && Rufous == "Low") || (Base == "Cream" && Rufous == "High"))) && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Mycena crocea (walnut mycena). A yellow mushroom with a pale stem and gills. Found only in eastern North America.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena|Light}^[((Base == "BlackTort" || Base == "Black") && Agouti == "Tabby" && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (SouthAm)]
      desc = Mycena luxaeterna (eternal light mushroom). A yellow mushroom with a green stem. Found only in a limited are of Atlantic rain forest in São Paulo, Brazil.
      {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Blue" || Base == "BlueTort") && (White == "MidWhite" || White == "HighWhite" || Albino == "Normal")) && (NorthAm || Europe || WestAs)]
      desc = Mycena maculata (reddish-spotted Mycena). A grey mushroom with light stem and gills. The fungus is found in North America and Europe (Germany and Norway). The North American distribution ranges north from Quebec, Canada, south to Mexico. It has also been recorded in Turkey.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Cream" || Base == "Fawn" || Base == "FawnTort") && (White == "LowWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) && (Europe)]
      desc = Mycena metata. A creamy-grey mushroom with a white trim to the cap. Found in Europe.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Cinnamon" || Base == "CinnamonTort" || (Base == "Red" && Rufous == "High")) && (White == "LowWhite" || White != "NpWhite") && Albino == "Normal") && (NewZeal)]
      desc = Mycena minirubra. A red mushroom with pale gills. Found only in New Zealand.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Black") && Agouti != "Tabby" && Albino == "Normal") && (Australia)]
      desc = Mycena nargan (Nargan's bonnet). A black mushroom, they start out covered in white scales. The species has been recorded from Tasmania, Victoria and southeastern South Australia.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Cream" || Base == "Fawn" || Base == "FawnTort") && (White == "LowWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) && (NorthAm || Europe || British)]
      desc = Mycena olivaceomarginata. A creamy-grey mushroom with a white trim to the cap. Found in Europe, Great Britain and North America.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena|{Fairyhelmet|Helmet}|Fuzzyfoot}^[((Base == "BlueTort" || Base == "FawnTort") && White == "LowWhite") && (NorthAm || Japan)]
      desc = Mycena overholtsii (snowbank fairy helmet, fuzzy foot). Grey mushroom with a pale cream stem. Found in western North America, particularly the Pacific Northwest, the Rocky Mountains and the Cascade mountains. It has been reported in four US states: South Dakota, California, Washington and Wyoming, but is not known in Oregon. It is also found in western Canada, and Hokkaido, Japan.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Black" || Base == "BlackTort") && Agouti == "Tabby" && White != "HighWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || Europe || British || Japan || Islands)]
      desc = Mycena polygramma (grooved bonnet). A brown mushroom with a frosty looking dark brown stem. Rare in North America, the fungus is common in Europe, including Great Britain. They have been collected on the Falkland Islands, and Japan.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Pink" || Base == "BlueTort") && White == "LowWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Mycena pura (lilac mycena, lilac bonnet). Pink or creamy pink mushroom. It is widely dispersed across North America.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Cinnamon" || Base == "Purple") && White == "LowWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || Europe || British || WestAs)]
      desc = Mycena purpureofusca (purple edge bonnet). Brown or purplish mushroom with white gills. In North America, the fungus has been recorded in North Carolina, Tennessee, New York, Michigan, Montana, Idaho, Washington, Oregon, California, Virginia, and South Dakota. In Canada, it has been found in Ontario. In Europe, it has been recorded from Britain, Scotland, the Czech Republic, Poland, Germany, Turkey and Austria.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Fawn" || Base == "FawnTort" || Base == "Cream") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (Europe || WestAs)]
      desc = Mycena renati (beautiful bonnet). A pinkish creamy mushroom. It has been collected in Austria, Uşak Province in Western Turkey, and Yugoslavia.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Fawn" || Base == "FawnTort" || Base == "Cream") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (Australia || NewZeal)]
      desc = Mycena roseoflava. A tiny cream mushroom with a yellow stem. It's bioluminescent, and known in New Zealand, Tasmania, and Victoria.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Fawn" || Base == "FawnTort" || Base == "Cream") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || MidAm || Europe || British || EastAs || Japan || Australia)]
      desc = Mycena sanguinolenta (bleeding bonnet, smaller bleeding Mycena, terrestrial bleeding Mycena). A creamy mushroom. It has been found from Maine to Washington and south to North Carolina and California in the United States, and from Nova Scotia to British Columbia in Canada. In Jamaica, it has been collected at an elevation of 1,800 m (5,900 ft). The distribution includes Europe (Britain, Germany, The Netherlands, Norway, Romania and Sweden) and Australia. In Asia, it has been collected from the alpine zone of the Changbai Mountains in Jilin Province, China, and from the provinces of Ōmi and Yamashiro in Japan.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena|{Fairyhelmet|Helmet}}^[((Base == "Red" || Base == "CinnamonTort") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || Europe)]
      desc = Mycena strobilinoides (flame mycena, scarlet fairy helmet). An orange mushroom with a light brown stem. The mushroom is more common in western than eastern North America, and is also present in Europe.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[(((Base == "Lilac") && (White == "LowWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) || Base == "White") && (NorthAm || Europe || British)]
      desc = Mycena stylobates (bulbous bonnet). A white mushroom, the stem may be grey. Collected in Tennessee, Michigan, Idaho, and Washington in the United States, and in Nova Scotia and Ontario in Canada. It is also found in Europe, including Britain, Denmark, Germany, Norway, Poland, Romania, Scotland, Serbia, Sweden.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Black" || Base == "Blue") && White == "LowWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Mycena subcaerulea. A dark grey mushroom, with a white-rimmed top. Mainly found in woods of the Adirondack Mountains in Upstate New York.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Black" || Base == "Chocolate" || Base == "BlackTort" || Base == "ChocolateTort") && (White == "LowWhite" || White == "NoWhite") && Albino == "Normal") && (Europe)]
      desc = Mycena tintinnabulum. A brown mushroom, with white gills. Found in Europe.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Lilac") && (White == "LowWhite" || White == "NoWhite")) && (NorthAm || British)]
      desc = Mycena urania (violet bonnet). A pale grey mushroom. Rare, it's found in Michigan, North Carolina, and Tennessee. Also reported in the Scottish Cairngorms.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "Blue" || Base == "BlueTort" || Base == "Purple") && White == "LowWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (Australia)]
      desc = Mycena vinacea. A purple mushroom with a white-rimmed cap and a grey stem. Found in Australia.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bonnet|Mycena}^[((Base == "LilacTort" || Base == "BlueTort") && (White == "LowWhite" || White == "NoWhite") && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || Europe || British)]
      desc = Mycena vitilis (snapping bonnet). A cream mushroom with a grey stem and white gills. The fungus is widely distributed in Europe (for example, Britain, Germany, Italy, Norway, Poland, Portugal) and North America.
      
    //Myrmecopterula
