      desc = Boletus curtisii. Bright yellow mushroom. Limited to eastern and southern North America. In the United States, it occurs from New England south to Florida, and west to Texas. Also reported from Mexico.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bolete|Cep|Bun|Porcino}^[(((Base == "Red" && Rufous == "Low") || (Base == "Cream" && Rufous == "High") || Base == "LilacTort" || Base == "Cinnamon" || Base == "CinnamonTort") && (Albino != "Normal" || White == "MidWhite")) && (NorthAm || Europe || NorthAf || SouthAf || WestAs || EastAs || Australia || NewZeal)]
      desc = Boletus edulis (cep, penny bun, porcino). Golden to brown mushroom with a white stem. It is common in Europe — from northern Scandinavia, south to the extremities of Greece and Italy — and North America, where its southern range extends as far south as Mexico. The European distribution extends north to Scandinavia and south to southern Italy and Morocco. In China, the mushroom can be found from the northeastern Heilongjiang to the Yunnan–Guizhou Plateau and Tibet. It has been recorded in Sagarmatha National Park in Nepal, as well as in the Indian forests of Arunachal Pradesh. In West Asia, the species has been reported from the northwest forests of Iran. Non-native populations have been found in areas of Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, and Zimbabwe.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Bolete|King}^[((Base == "Cinnamon" || Base == "CinnamonTort") && Albino == "Normal" && (White == "LowWhite" || White == "NoWhite")) && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Boletus fibrillosus (fib king). Brown & gold mushroom with pale yellow pores. Found in western North America.
