    {Mushroom^0.5|Chanterelle}^[((Base == "Red") && (Albino != "Normal" || White == "HighWhite")) && (NorthAm || SouthAm)]
      desc = Gloeocantharellus purpurascens (Indian Creek mushroom, violet-staining chanterelle). Orange-brown mushroom with a pale yellow stem. In the United States it is found in the Great Smoky Mountains National Park in the Appalachian Mountains in North Carolina. It also grows in the subtropical cloud forests in Mexico, having been found in the provinces of Oaxaca,Veracruz and Guerrero. Also known in French Guiana.
    //Gomphus
    {Mushroom^0.5|Chanterelle|{Pig's-ear{s|}|Pigear{s|}}}^[((Base == "FawnTort" || Base == "LilacTort" || Base == "Cream") && Albino == "Normal" && (White == "NoWhite")) && (NorthAm || Europe || WestAs || EastAs || SEastAs || Japan)]
      desc = Gomphus clavatus (pig's ears, violet chanterelle). Cream mushroom with a pale purple-pink underside. In Asia, reported from China, Japan, Korea, Malaysia, Nepal, and Pakistan. European countries where the fungus has been reported include Austria, the Czech Republic, France, Germany, Greece, Italy, Lithuania, Poland, Romania, Russia, Sweden, Switzerland, and Turkey. In North America, the fungus has been found across Canada, Mexico, and the United States, being abundant in the Pacific Northwest.
    //Phaeoclavulina
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(Base == "White" || Base == "Cream") && (NorthAm || Europe || British)]
      desc = Phaeoclavulina abietina (green-staining coral). Pale yellow branching mushroom. In North America, it is found in the United States, including the Pacific Northwest, as well as Mexico. It is also found in Europe.
    //Ramaria
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(Base == "White" || Base == "Cream") && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Ramaria acrisiccescens (blah coral). Pale yellow branching mushroom. It has been recorded from Washington and California.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(Base == "Pink" || ((Base == "Cinnamon" || (Base == "Red" && Rufous == "High")) && Albino == "Normal" && White == "NoWhite")) && (NorthAm || EastAs)]
      desc = Ramaria araiospora (red coral). Bright red-pink branching mushroom, the colour fades to pale pink over time. Primarily known from the Pacific Northwest region of North America, although it has been reported from Kansas. Has been collected in the eastern Himalayas and Mexico.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(((Base == "Red" && Rufous == "Low") || (Base == "Cream" && Rufous == "High")) && Albino == "Normal" && (White == "NoWhite")) && (NorthAm || Europe)]
      desc = Ramaria aurea. Pale golden branching coral mushroom. It is found in North America and Europe.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(Base == "Pink") && (NorthAm || MidAm || SouthAm || Europe || NorthAf || WestAs || EastAs || Japan || Australia)]
      desc = Ramaria botrytis (clustered coral, pink-tipped coral mushroom, cauliflower coral). Pink coral mushroom with darker tips. Found in Africa (Tunisia), Australia, Chile, Asia (including the eastern Himalayas of India, Nepal, Japan, Korea, Pakistan, China, the Far East of Russia, and Turkey) and Europe (including the Netherlands, France, Portugal, Italy, Bulgaria, and Spain). It is widely distributed in North America, where it is most common in the southeast and along the Pacific Coast but also appears in Mexico and Guatemala.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(Base == "Pink" && (White == "MidWhite" || White == "HighWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Ramaria botrytoides. Pale pink coral mushroom with a white base. Found in the eastern United States.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(Base == "Cream" && (White == "LowWhite" && Albino == "Normal")) && (Australia)]
      desc = Ramaria capitata. Cream coral fungus with white tips. It is found in Australia, where it is widespread and relatively common.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(((Base == "Red" && Rufous == "Low") || (Base == "Cream" && Rufous == "High")) && Albino == "Normal" && (White == "NoWhite")) && (NorthAm || SouthAm || NorthAf || WestAs || Japan || SEastAs || NewZeal || Islands)]
      desc = Ramaria cokeri. Light golden coral fungus. Orignally found in the Appalachian Mountains in the United States. The species has been reported from Japan, Mexico, Colombia, Malaysia, Indonesia, Sri Lanka, Pakistan, Papua New Guinea, Solomon Islands, and New Zealand. In 2012, it was reported for the first time from the Canary Islands and Guinea.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(((Base == "Cream" && Rufous == "High")) && Albino == "Normal" && (White == "NoWhite")) && (NorthAm || Europe)]
      desc = Ramaria decurrens (ochre coral). Pale yellow coral fungus. It is found in Europe and North America. 
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[((Base == "Lilac" || Base == "Fawn" || Base == "Cream") && (White == "MidWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) && (NorthAm || Europe || Australia)]
      desc = Ramaria fennica (bitter coral). Warm grey coral fungus with a white base. It is found in Australia, Europe and North America. 
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral|Changle}^[(((Base == "Cream" && Rufous == "High")) && Albino == "Normal" && (White == "NoWhite")) && (SouthAm || Europe)]
      desc = Ramaria flava (changle). Pale yellow coral fungus. Found in Europe. Also native to temperate areas of southern Chile and south of Brazil (state of Rio Grande do Sul). 
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[((Base == "Red") && Albino == "Normal" && (White == "NoWhite")) && (Europe)]
      desc = Ramaria flavescens. Pale orange coral fungus. Found in Europe.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral|Clavaria|Fungus}^[((Base == "Cream") && (White == "MidWhite" || White == "LowWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) && (Europe || Islands)]
      desc = Ramaria formosa (pinkish coral mushroom, salmon coral, beautiful clavaria, handsome clavaria, yellow-tipped- or pink coral fungus). Pale yellow coral fungus with a pale pink & white base. Found in Europe, including Cyprus.
    {Mushroom^0.5|Coral}^[(((Base == "Red" && Rufous == "Low") || (Base == "Cream" && Rufous == "High")) && (White == "MidWhite" || White == "LowWhite" || Albino != "Normal")) && (NorthAm || Europe)]
