    {Lichen|Cladonia}^[((Base == "Green") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (Europe || British || NorthAm || WestAs || EastAs)]
      desc = Cladonia parasitica (fence-rail cladonia, fence-rail cup lichen, parasite club lichen). A leafy lichen growing on wood. Found in North America and Eurasia.
    {Lichen|Cladonia}^[((Base == "Green")) && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Cladonia perforata (Florida perforate cladonia, Florida perforate reindeer lichen). Pale green shrub-like lichen found in California and Florida.
    {Lichen|Moss}^[((Base == "White")) && (NorthAm || Europe || British || WestAs || EastAs)]
      desc = Cladonia rangiferina (reindeer cup lichen, reindeer lichen, grey reindeer lichen, reindeer moss, deer moss, caribou moss). Moss-like pale grey lichen. Found across the arctic tundra in the northern hemisphere, as well as more temperate areas.
    Lichen^[((Base == "Green") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || SouthAm || Europe || NorthAf || WestAs || EastAs || Australia || NewZeal)]
      desc = Cladonia rei (wand lichen). Green lichen growing as stalks. It is a widespread species, with a range extending to Europe, Africa, Asia, Australasia, North America, and South America. It is widely distributed in Europe, having been recorded in Austria, Belgium and Luxemburg, Czech Republic, Estonia, Lithuania, Norway, Poland, Slovakia, Spain, and Sweden. More recently, it was reported from Armenia, from the coastal sand-dunes of Portugal, and from the Pyrénées-Orientales in the French Mediterranean region. Its Australasian distribution includes both Australia and New Zealand. It was reported as new to India. It was recorded from Iran and Turkey.
    Lichen^[((Base == "Green") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || EastAs)]
      desc = Cladonia sobolescens (peg lichen). Green leafy lichen. Found in temperate eastern North America and East Asia. The northern limit of its North American range extends to Prince Edward Island in Canada.
    Lichen^[((Base == "White" || Base == "Fawn" || Base == "Lilac")) && (NorthAm || SouthAm || Europe || EastAs || Antarctica)]
      desc = Cladonia squamosa (dragon cup lichen). Pale grey branching lichen. It is found in Europe, North and South America, Asia, and on King George Island in Antarctica.
    Lichen^[((Base == "White")) && (NorthAm || Europe || WestAs || EastAs)]
      desc = Cladonia stellaris (star-tipped cup lichen, star reindeer lichen). Pale green lichen growing as mats on soil or gravel. Found in boreal and arctic regions of the north hemisphere.
    Lichen^[((Base == "White" || (Base == "Cream" && (Albino != "Normal" || White != "NoWhite")))) && (NorthAm || Europe || WestAs || Islands)]
      desc = Cladonia strepsilis (olive cup lichen). White and pale yellow frilly lichen. It is found in Asia, Europe and North America. In Iceland, it is only found in a few locations in the Eastern Region.
    Lichen^[((Base == "Green") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || MidAm || SouthAm || NorthAf || SouthAf || WestAs || EastAs || SEastAs || Australia || NewZeal)]
      desc = Cladonia subradiata. Pale green lichen growing as branching stalks. Widely distributed in Central and South America, extending to North Carolina in North America. In South America the lichen has been collected from Brazil, Chile, Argentina, Paraguay, and Guyana. It has also been recorded from Asia, Africa, Papua New Guinea, New Caledonia, Australia, and New Zealand.
    //Coenogonium
    Lichen^[((Base == "Green") && White == "LowWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || Europe || WestAs || EastAs || Australia)]
      desc = Coenogonium pineti (pink dimple lichen). Grey-green lichen with pale creamy discs, growing on bark or mosses. The lichen has a widespread distribution in cooler, temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere, but has also been recorded from southeastern mainland Australia and Tasmania.
    //Collema
    Lichen^[((Base == "Chocolate" || Base == "Black") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm || SouthAm || Europe || Australia || Antarctica)]
      desc = Collema coccophorum (soil pulp lichen, jelly lichen). Dark brown lichen. Found in many areas of the world, including North America, Eastern Europe and Norway, Australia, and South America. It has also been recorded in Antarctica.
    Lichen^[((Base == "Black") && Agouti != "Tabby" && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Collema nigrescens (button jelly lichen, bat's wing lichen). Black lichen growing on bark in coastal California.
    //Cryptothecia
    Lichen^[((Base == "Lilac" || Base == "Fawn") && White == "NoWhite" && Albino == "Normal") && (SEastAs)]
      desc = Cryptothecia punctosorediata. Pale grey-green lichen growing on bark in northern Thailand.
    Lichen^[(Base == "Pink" || Base == "Red") && (NorthAm || MidAm || SouthAm || SouthAf)]
      desc = Cryptothecia rubrocincta. Red-pink & white lichen growing on dead wood. Widely distributed in the southeastern United States; in 1954 the north border of its distribution was given as a line passing through southern Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama and Georgia. The northern limit is thought to be North Carolina. In North Carolina, it is found on Smith Island. Aalso widespread in tropical and subtropical areas of the West Indies and Central and South America. In South America it is found north of Chile and Argentina. It is rarer in Africa, having only been collected from three geographically widely separated mountain regions: São Tomé and Príncipe, Tanzania, and DR Congo.
    Lichen^[(Base == "BlackTort" || (Base == "Black" && (Albino == "Point" || (Agouti == "Tabby" && Gold != "None")))) && (NorthAm)]
      desc = Cyphelium tigillare (yellow soot lichen). Lichen growing as yellow-green spots with a black center on wood. Widespread in North America.

